

===================================================
        DOUBLY CIRCULAR LINKED LIST
===================================================


1. What is Doubly Circular Linked List?

    - A Doubly Circular Linked List is a linear data
      structure in which elements are stored in the
      form of nodes.

    - Each node contains three parts: data, next
      reference and Previous reference.

    - Elements are not stored in continuous memory
      locations.

    - Each node is connected to the next node using
      the next reference.

    - Each node is also connected to the previous
      node using the Previous reference.

    - The last node points back to the first node
      (head), forming a circular connection.

    - The first node points back to the last node
      (tail), forming a circular connection in the
      backward direction.

    - We can traverse the Linked List in both
      forward and backward directions.

    - Doubly Circular Linked List has a dynamic size,
      meaning we can add or remove nodes at runtime.


NOTE:

    - Whenever a new Node is created, its next and
      Previous references are initially null.

    - We establish connections between nodes after
      the new Node is created.

    - In a Doubly Circular Linked List, the last
      node's next reference always points to head.

    - The first node's Previous reference always
      points to tail.

    - There is no null reference at either end when
      the list is non-empty.


Syntax:

    class Node {

        int data;

        Node next;

        Node Previous;

    }


===================================================
2. What is Head?
===================================================

    - Head is a reference variable that stores the
      reference (address) of the first node in the
      Doubly Circular Linked List.

    - Initially, head is null because the Linked
      List is empty.

    - After inserting the first node, head points
      to that node.

    - Head continues to point to the first node
      even when new nodes are added at the end.

    - The first node's Previous reference points
      to tail.


===================================================
3. What is Tail?
===================================================

    - Tail is a reference variable that stores the
      reference (address) of the last node in the
      Doubly Circular Linked List.

    - Initially, tail is null.

    - After inserting the first node, both head
      and tail point to the same node.

    - Whenever a new node is added at the end,
      tail gets updated to point to the new last node.

    - The last node's next reference always points
      back to head.

    - The last node's Previous reference points
      to the second-last node when more than one
      node exists.


IMPORTANT:

    tail.next = head;

    head.Previous = tail;


    - tail.next = head creates the circular
      connection in the forward direction.

    - head.Previous = tail creates the circular
      connection in the backward direction.


===================================================
4. Structure of Doubly Circular Linked List
===================================================

Example:

                  head                         tail
                    |                            |
                    v                            v

               [10] <----> [20] <----> [30]
                 ^                           |
                 |___________________________|


Remember:

    - Head points to the first node (10).

    - Tail points to the last node (30).

    - Every node's next stores the reference of
      the next node.

    - Every node's Previous stores the reference
      of the previous node.

    - The last node's next points back to head.

    - The first node's Previous points back to tail.

    - There is no null reference at either end
      when the list is non-empty.


===================================================
5. Working of Doubly Circular Linked List
===================================================

    1. First, a new node is created using the
       new keyword.

    2. The constructor initializes its data and
       sets both next and Previous to null.

    3. If the Linked List is empty (head == null):

           - Both head and tail point to the new node.

           - Set head.next = tail to create the
             forward circular connection.

           - Set head.Previous = tail to create
             the backward circular connection.

    4. If the Linked List is not empty:

           - Connect the new node's Previous to
             the current tail.

           - Connect the new node's next to head.

           - Connect the current tail's next to
             the new node.

           - Update tail to point to the new node.

           - Connect head's Previous to the new tail.

    5. Increase size by 1.


IMPORTANT:

    In a Doubly Circular Linked List, we maintain
    two circular connections:

        Forward Circular Connection:

            tail.next = head;


        Backward Circular Connection:

            head.Previous = tail;


    Both connections are necessary to maintain
    the circular structure in a Doubly Circular
    Linked List.

